PATH RECONCILIATION (binding, honest)
  The generated prompt's Output/Allowed path is
  `apps/apple/TeslaSync/Features/Settings/**`. Unlike the earlier
  ActiveSessions log (which relocated to `Sources/Features/Settings`), the
  CURRENT project.yml DOES compile `TeslaSync/Features` (the iOS + macOS app
  targets list it as a source root), and the five most-recent shipped P7 pages
  at HEAD all live under `TeslaSync/Features/<Category>/`
  (Notifications/QuietHours, BrowserNotifications, Channels, AlertRules,
  Archived). The page is therefore implemented exactly where the prompt mandates
  AND where the live convention puts it: apps/apple/TeslaSync/Features/Settings/**.
  No AppRoute/AppRouteParser/TeslaSyncApp edits were needed (see Navigation).

SCOPE (honesty covenant: no scope-narrowing, no stubs as final, ADR-011)
  The web source PrivacyPage.tsx is a thin PageContainer (title + subtitle +
  copyLink) wrapping <PrivacySection/>. The thin page manifest lists only the 2
  page strings, but a title-only screen would be a stub. The FULL functional
  surface of PrivacySection is reproduced natively:
    - the cyan ShieldCheck header (icon + privacy.title + privacy.subtitle),
    - "Recently viewed pages": title + body + live pluralized stored-count +
      a destructive Clear gated behind a warning confirmation that honours the
      web `silenceKey` ("clear-recent-pages": auto-resolve when previously
      silenced; a "Don't ask again" confirm action), with success toast,
    - "Cookies & analytics consent" (ALWAYS rendered, even when consent is
      gated off, per the web comment): switched bodyOn/bodyOff copy driven by
      require_cookie_consent, the current-state label (accepted/declined/
      unknown), and Re-grant / Withdraw / Reset, each disabled when already in
      that state, each with a success toast.
  Browser-local state is modeled through injected seams (UserDefaults in
  production; in-memory doubles for previews) — no persistence logic in the view
  (ADR-004). The experimental `feature-views/PrivacySection.*` component is a
  SEPARATE parity unit using its own `Privacy*` types; this P7 PAGE is
  self-contained and uses DISTINCT `AccountPrivacy*` type names to avoid any
  collision (see DUPLICATE-SYMBOL NOTE).

